'''''Ixia viridiflora''''', also known as '''turquoise ixia''', is a tall member of the genus ''[[Ixia]]''. It comes from around the [[Tulbagh]] in South Africa, [[Cape Province]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Duncan |first=Graham |date=2003 |title=ENDANGERED GEOPHYTES OF THE CAPE FLORAL KINGDOM |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/45065597 |journal=Curtis's Botanical Magazine |volume=20 |issue=4 |pages=245–250 |issn=1355-4905}}</ref> It has small [[corm]]s under the ground.

This corn lily is very rare. Its [[habitat]]s are often destroyed by human influence. As a consequence, its conservation status is vulnerable and is tending to worsen.

The name turquoise ixia refers to the blue-green [[Turquoise (color)|turquoise colour]] of the flowers, which is a rare colour for flowers. They are grouped in long inflorescences and are normally star-shaped as in most corn lilies. The flowers have a black-purple centre. The [[ovary (botany)|ovary]] is 3-locular. The flower is pollinated by specific [[Scarabaeidae|scarab beetle]]s known as [[monkey beetle]]s of the tribe [[Hopliini]].

The turquoise ixia has good ornamental traits but it is rarely [[Plant cultivation|cultivated]] due to its conservation status.

